<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Helen of Troy</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Helen of Sparta</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">In Greek mythology, Helen was the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> and Leda and the wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelaus">Menelaus</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>. Her abduction or flight with the Trojan prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> caused the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trojan-war">Trojan War</a>, told in the Homeric epics. She was worshipped as a goddess in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Laconia</a>, notably at the Menelaion at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/therapne">Therapne</a> near Sparta.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/helen-of-troy.jpg" alt="Helen of Troy" width="320" height="656" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Helen_of_Sparta_boards_a_ship_for_Troy_fresco_from_the_House_of_the_Tragic_Poet_in_Pompeii.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Helen is a figure of Greek mythology said to have been the most beautiful woman in the world. She was believed to be the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> by Leda or Nemesis, and the sister of Clytemnestra, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castor-and-pollux">Castor and Pollux</a>. In her youth she was abducted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theseus">Theseus</a>, who left her with his mother Aethra or an associate at Aphidnae or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, until her brothers Castor and Pollux invaded Athens and returned her to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>.</p><p>When she came to marry, kings and princes from around the world sought her hand. Her father Tyndareus feared giving offence to those he rejected, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odysseus">Odysseus</a> proposed that all the suitors swear to defend the chosen husband against anyone who quarrelled with him. This was the Oath of Tyndareus. Menelaus was chosen because he had offered the most gifts, and he and Helen ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> after Tyndareus and Leda abdicated. They had a daughter, Hermione.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, a Trojan prince, came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> on a supposed diplomatic mission. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aphrodite">Aphrodite</a> had promised him the most beautiful woman in the world as a reward for judging her the most beautiful of the goddesses. Most ancient sources, following <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>, hold that Helen fell in love with him and went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a> willingly, though she is sometimes shown as abducted. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelaus">Menelaus</a> called on the other suitors to honour their oaths, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trojan-war">Trojan War</a> began.</p><p>Accounts of her time in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a> contradict one another. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> shows her as ambivalent, regretful of her choice yet sly in restoring her reputation, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a>'s Aeneid has her feign Bacchic rites and signal to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> from a tower. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euripides">Euripides</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stesichorus">Stesichorus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> denied that she went to Troy at all and placed her in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> for the war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>'s account Helen returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelaus">Menelaus</a>. In another version, used by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euripides">Euripides</a> in Orestes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> saved her and she was taken up to Mount Olympus, becoming a sea goddess who watched over sailors with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castor-and-pollux">Castor and Pollux</a>. Her major cult centres were in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Laconia</a>, and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/therapne">Therapne</a> she shared a shrine with Menelaus. She was also worshipped in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a> and on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>. Images of her appear from the 7th century BCE, and the elopement or abduction was a popular motif in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/classical-greece">Classical Greece</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Helen of Troy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The region had been important in the Mycenaean age: a palace at Ayios Vasileios and a mansion at the Menelaion near the later city date from the Late Bronze Age, and Homer made Lacedaemon the kingdom of Menelaus and <b>Helen</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sparta">Sparta · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Homer remembered Laconia as the kingdom of Menelaus and <b>Helen</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/mycenaean-kingdom-of-laconia">Mycenaean Kingdom of Laconia</a></li></ul></section>
